QB.push(
{id:1231,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A mobile app lets users attach photos. iPhone uploads arrive as `.heic` files and every request containing one is rejected with `400 invalid_request_error`. Android JPEG uploads work fine. What is the correct fix?",
o:["A. Send the HEIC bytes but declare `media_type: \"image/jpeg\"`, since the decoder sniffs the real format anyway","B. Transcode the file client-side to one of the four supported formats before building the `image` block","C. Switch the block to a `url` source, which accepts any format the browser can render","D. Upload it through the Files API, which normalises arbitrary formats on ingest"],a:1,
e:"Vision accepts exactly four formats: **JPEG, PNG, GIF, WebP** (`image/jpeg`, `image/png`, `image/gif`, `image/webp`). HEIC is not among them, and no source type widens the list, because the constraint is the image decoder rather than the transport. The `media_type` you declare must match the actual bytes; mislabelling does not make an unsupported codec readable. The transferable principle is that format normalisation belongs at the edge of your pipeline: decode once on upload into a supported format, then every downstream consumer sees a uniform input.",
w:"A — the declared `media_type` must match the real bytes, and relabelling an unsupported codec does not make it decodable. C — the `url` source only changes how bytes reach the API, not which formats are accepted. D — the Files API stores and serves the file you gave it; it performs no format conversion."},

{id:1232,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A QA tool sends an animated GIF that cycles through four states of a loading spinner and asks Claude to describe the animation sequence. Claude describes only a single static state and never mentions motion. Why, and what is the fix?",
o:["A. GIF is unsupported; convert the file to PNG before sending","B. `max_tokens` is too low to describe four states, so the answer is truncated","C. Animations are unsupported and only the first frame is used; extract the representative frames yourself and send them as separate labelled `image` blocks","D. Send it as a `document` block, which rasterises every frame in order"],a:2,
e:"GIF and WebP are supported *formats*, but **animation is not supported: only the first frame is used**. Claude therefore genuinely never sees frames two through four. The fix is client-side frame extraction: decode the animation, pick the frames that matter, and send them as separate `image` blocks each introduced by a short text label such as `Frame 1:`. That also gives you control over how many visual tokens the sequence costs, since every frame is billed as its own image.",
w:"A — GIF is a supported format; the limitation is animation, and converting to a single PNG loses the other frames just the same. B — truncation would show as `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"`; here the extra frames were never in the input. D — `document` blocks are for PDFs and plain text, not animated images, and nothing server-side unrolls animation frames."},

{id:1233,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A regression-testing team wants Claude to review a 30-second screen recording (MP4) of a checkout flow and report where the layout breaks. What is the correct approach?",
o:["A. Video is not a supported input; sample frames client-side at the interesting moments and send them as labelled `image` blocks in one message","B. Upload the MP4 through the Files API and reference it by `file_id`, which enables video understanding","C. Convert the MP4 to an animated GIF, which Claude can play back","D. Send the MP4 as a `document` block so the frames are extracted server-side"],a:0,
e:"Claude's multimodal input is **images and documents only; there is no video input**. Any video workflow is really a frame-sampling workflow that you own: decide a sampling policy (fixed interval, scene change, or event-triggered), extract those frames, label each with an adjacent text block, and send them in a single user turn so Claude can reason across them. The transferable principle is to convert an unsupported modality into a supported one deterministically in your own code, where you control cost, rather than hoping the API will do it.",
w:"B — the Files API is transport and storage; it does not add a modality, and there is no video understanding to unlock. C — animated formats are read as a single frame, so a GIF conveys even less than a handful of stills. D — `document` blocks cover PDFs and plain text; no server-side video decoding exists."},

{id:1234,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"How many visual tokens does a 1000x1000 px image consume, and by what rule?",
o:["A. About 1333, from `(width x height) / 750`","B. 1296, because cost is one visual token per 28x28-pixel patch: `⌈1000/28⌉ x ⌈1000/28⌉` = 36 x 36","C. 1000, one visual token per row of pixels","D. 1568, because every image is billed at the standard tier's full visual-token budget"],a:1,
e:"Claude sees images as **patches, not pixels**. Each patch is a 28x28-pixel block and costs one visual token, so an image costs `⌈width/28⌉ x ⌈height/28⌉`. For 1000x1000 that is 36 x 36 = **1296**. **Currency note:** the older `(width x height)/750` approximation is gone; it happens to land near the patch count for square mid-size images, which is exactly what makes it a dangerous thing to keep in a cost model. Always use the ceiling-of-28 formula, and remember it is computed on the dimensions Claude actually processes, after any downscaling.",
w:"A — `(w x h)/750` is the obsolete formula; it coincidentally lands close here but diverges badly for other aspect ratios and sizes. C — nothing in the pricing model is per row of pixels. D — the budget is a ceiling that caps oversized images, not a flat charge applied to every image."},

{id:1235,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A document pipeline sends 3840x2160 page renders. After migrating from an older model to a newer one, image input spend roughly triples while page count, prompt, and code are unchanged. What is the economic explanation?",
o:["A. The newer tokenizer produces about 30% more tokens per unit of input","B. `max_tokens` was raised during the migration, inflating input billing","C. Prompt caching silently stopped applying to image blocks on the new model","D. The newer model is on the high-resolution tier, so an image that was previously downscaled to about 1560 visual tokens can now cost up to 4784; downsample before sending if the extra fidelity is not needed"],a:3,
e:"Each model sits in a **resolution tier** defined by a long-edge limit and a visual-token budget: roughly 1568 px / 1568 tokens on the standard tier, and about 2576 px / 4784 tokens on the newest high-resolution models. A 4K page that the standard tier squeezed to 1456x819 (about 1560 tokens) survives at 2576x1449 on the high-resolution tier and costs the full 4784. Nothing is broken; you are now buying fidelity you did not ask for. **Currency note:** high-resolution support is automatic on the newer models, with no beta header, so a model swap is a silent cost change. Downsample deliberately when the extra detail earns nothing.",
w:"A — tokenizer changes affect text, not the patch arithmetic that prices images. B — `max_tokens` caps output and does not appear in input billing at all. C — caching affects whether you pay 0.1x on a repeated prefix, and losing it would not produce a clean 3x on fresh page renders."},

{id:1236,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A model's native image resolution is described as a pair of limits. Which pair?",
o:["A. A maximum edge length in pixels and a maximum visual-token count, whichever binds first","B. A maximum edge length in pixels only","C. A maximum file size in megabytes only","D. A maximum megapixel count and a maximum aspect ratio"],a:0,
e:"Each tier publishes **two** limits: a **max long edge** (about 1568 px standard, 2576 px high-resolution) and a **max visual-token budget** (1568 standard, 4784 high-resolution). Claude finds the largest aspect-preserving size that satisfies *both*, then processes that. Knowing there are two is the practical point: an image can sit comfortably under the edge limit and still be downscaled because its patch count `⌈w/28⌉ x ⌈h/28⌉` exceeds the token budget. Teams that model only the edge limit compute the wrong processed dimensions and misalign every coordinate they get back.",
w:"B — the edge limit alone is the classic half-truth; the token budget is what actually binds for most photos and screenshots. C — file size limits exist (10 MB per image on the Claude API) but are a separate rejection, not the definition of native resolution. D — neither megapixels nor aspect ratio is expressed as a limit; both are consequences of the edge and token limits."},

{id:1237,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An extraction service scans A4 pages at 130 DPI, producing 1075x1520 px images, and asks Claude for bounding boxes around each table. Both dimensions are under 1568 px, yet every box is offset by a consistent ratio when drawn on the original scan. What happened?",
o:["A. Claude returned normalised coordinates in the 0-to-1 range, which must be multiplied by the page size","B. Padding shifted the coordinate origin away from the top-left corner","C. The visual-token budget was exceeded: `39 x 55` = 2145 tokens against a 1568-token standard-tier budget, so the page was downscaled to 924x1307 and coordinates refer to that size","D. Bounding boxes are only supported on `document` blocks, not `image` blocks"],a:2,
e:"This is the most common cause of misaligned coordinates. The **visual-token budget can trigger a resize even when neither side exceeds the edge limit**. At 1075x1520 the patch count is `⌈1075/28⌉ x ⌈1520/28⌉` = 39 x 55 = 2145, above the standard tier's 1568, so Claude scales the page to 924x1307 and reports pixel positions in *that* image. Either pre-resize to the computed size so coordinates map one-to-one, or recover the resized dimensions with the reference resize helper and rescale. A high-resolution-tier model, with its 4784-token budget, would not have resized this scan at all.",
w:"A — Claude returns absolute pixel coordinates, and asking for normalised ranges actively degrades accuracy. B — padding is applied only to the bottom and right edges, so the origin never moves; padding causes a small scaling error only if you normalise by padded dimensions. D — `document` blocks are worse for coordinates, because PDF pages are rasterised server-side at dimensions you do not control."},

{id:1238,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A form-field locator normalises the pixel coordinates Claude returns by dividing by the dimensions of the image as Claude perceived it including padding. Every point is off by a small, consistent fraction. What is the correct normalisation?",
o:["A. Divide by the original pre-upload dimensions, since Claude corrects internally for any resize","B. Divide by the resized dimensions, not the padded ones: Claude pads every image up to the next multiple of 28 px on the bottom and right edges, and that padding contains no content","C. Divide by 1568 on both axes, the standard-tier edge limit","D. Ask Claude to return normalised coordinates so no division is needed"],a:1,
e:"After resizing, Claude **pads to the next multiple of 28 px on the bottom and right only** so the image tiles cleanly into patches. A 924x1307 page becomes 924x1316. That padding is empty: page content only ever occupies the un-padded resized region, so dividing by 1316 scales every y coordinate by 1307/1316. Because padding is bottom-and-right only, the origin never moves and a per-axis linear rescale is exact. The principle: **normalise by the resized dimensions**, and clamp returned points to those dimensions before rescaling.",
w:"A — the original dimensions are wrong unless no resize occurred; Claude reports positions in the image it processed. C — 1568 is a limit, not the image's dimensions, and the resized size is almost never square. D — Claude works poorly when asked for normalised coordinates; always request pixels and normalise yourself."},

{id:1239,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A UI-automation pipeline pre-resizes screenshots to exactly the size the model processes, so returned click points need no conversion. Weeks later a new capture device changes the source resolution and clicks start landing slightly off, with no error anywhere. What guards against this class of silent drift?",
o:["A. Set `disable_parallel_tool_use` so only one screenshot is in flight at a time","B. Query the Files API for each image's stored dimensions before sending","C. Use `url` sources so that every image is resized by the same server-side path","D. Mark each image block with `transformations: {\"oversized_image\": \"error\"}` so a request whose marked image would be resized is rejected with a 400 naming the image's dimensions and the largest size that fits"],a:3,
e:"Pre-resizing only protects coordinates while your pipeline keeps producing the right sizes. A new image source, or a model on a different resolution tier, quietly reintroduces server-side resizing. Setting **`transformations: {\"oversized_image\": \"error\"}`** on the image block converts that silent drift into a visible `400 invalid_request_error` that names both the offending dimensions and the target to rescale to. The default, `\"downsize\"`, keeps the automatic behaviour. The transferable principle: when a value is load-bearing, make violation loud rather than lossy.",
w:"A — parallel tool use governs how many tools Claude may call at once and has nothing to do with image geometry. B — dimensions alone do not tell you whether *this* model's tier would resize them, which is precisely the check the setting performs. C — `url` sources are resized by the same rule as base64; the source type changes nothing about geometry."},

{id:1240,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"If the `transformations` field is omitted from an image content block, what oversized-image behaviour applies?",
o:["A. `\"downsize\"`, aspect-preserving automatic resizing, and the setting is per image block","B. `\"error\"`, so oversized images are rejected by default","C. There is no default; the field is required for any image over 1568 px","D. `\"crop\"`, so the image is centre-cropped to the tier limit"],a:0,
e:"The default is **`\"oversized_image\": \"downsize\"`**: Claude scales the image, preserving aspect ratio, to the largest size satisfying both the tier's edge limit and its visual-token budget. The setting is **per image block**, checked only against its own value, so one request can mix an image whose exact dimensions matter (a screenshot you will click on, marked `\"error\"`) with images where resizing is harmless (a logo, left at the default). Nothing is ever cropped, and content is never discarded: only downscaling and bottom-right padding occur.",
w:"B — `\"error\"` is the opt-in behaviour you request explicitly, not the default. C — the field is entirely optional; oversized images are handled silently unless you say otherwise. D — cropping would discard content and is not part of the resize pipeline."},

{id:1241,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team explicitly sets `transformations: {\"oversized_image\": \"downsize\"}` on a 9000x6000 px JPEG of about 4 MB, expecting automatic resizing. The request is still rejected. Why?",
o:["A. `\"downsize\"` only applies to `base64` sources, and this image was sent by URL","B. The maximum dimension of 8000 px on the longest side is a separate hard limit, and the `oversized_image` setting never lets an image past it","C. The image exceeded the 10 MB per-image base64 ceiling","D. Automatic resizing only applies to PNG and WebP images"],a:1,
e:"There are two distinct classes of size rule. Tier limits (edge length and visual-token budget) drive **resizing**, which `oversized_image` governs. Absolute ceilings, notably the **8000 px maximum on the longest side** and the stricter per-image dimension limit that applies to many-image requests, drive **rejection**, and no `transformations` value lets an image past them. At 9000 px the long edge breaks the hard ceiling before resizing is ever considered. The transferable principle: distinguish limits that trigger a transformation from limits that terminate the request.",
w:"A — the setting applies to every source type; how bytes arrive only affects whether the error names the image's position. C — at roughly 4 MB the file is comfortably inside the 10 MB per-image limit, so size is not the trigger here. D — resizing is format-independent across the four supported formats."},

{id:1242,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An OCR product highlights extracted fields in its own PDF viewer using bounding boxes from Claude. With native `document` blocks the boxes are unusable. What is the right architecture?",
o:["A. Enable citations on the document block so coordinates are returned per page","B. Ask for normalised coordinates between 0 and 1000 and scale them to page size","C. Rasterise the pages to images in your own code at dimensions you choose, send `image` blocks, and pre-resize so returned pixel coordinates map one-to-one","D. Set `transformations` on the `document` block so page rasterisation is skipped"],a:2,
e:"With native PDF support, **pages are rasterised server-side at dimensions you do not control**, so the pixel coordinates Claude returns cannot be reliably mapped back onto the page. Coordinate work therefore requires owning the rasterisation: render pages at a known size, pre-resize to the size the model will actually process, and use the returned pixels directly. You trade away the extracted text layer that native PDF support supplies alongside each page image, so weigh that against the coordinate fidelity you gain.",
w:"A — citations attach source references to claims; they do not produce pixel geometry. B — Claude performs poorly on normalised coordinate requests; always ask for absolute pixels and normalise in your own code. D — the `document` block does not accept `transformations`, precisely because its page dimensions are chosen server-side."},

{id:1243,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"What is the recommended way to ask Claude for the location of an element in an image, and to get it in a machine-readable form?",
o:["A. Ask for a percentage of image width and height, which is resolution-independent","B. Ask for coordinates on a normalised 0-to-1000 grid, then scale them","C. Let Claude choose whatever convention it prefers and parse the prose","D. Ask explicitly for absolute pixel coordinates, then normalise in your own code, and bind the response shape with a structured-output schema such as an `[x1, y1, x2, y2]` array per element"],a:3,
e:"Claude works best with **absolute pixel coordinates**, requested explicitly in the prompt, and works poorly when asked for normalised ranges. Do any normalisation yourself, dividing by the dimensions of the image you sent. To get geometry as reliable JSON rather than prose, declare a typed schema with **structured outputs** (`output_config.format` of type `json_schema`), which uses constrained decoding to guarantee a schema-valid response. Coordinates remain approximate, so state the expected format in the prompt and spot-check visually before scaling up.",
w:"A — percentages are a normalised form, and asking for them degrades accuracy just as a 0-to-1000 grid does. B — the normalised 0-to-1000 convention is specifically called out as something Claude does not handle well. C — leaving the convention unstated is the least reliable option and forces brittle prose parsing."},

{id:1244,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A computer-use agent captures the desktop at 2560x1440 and returns the PNG inside a `tool_result` block. Instead of being downscaled like a normal oversized image, the request fails validation. What is going on?",
o:["A. `tool_result` images that exceed the model's limits are rejected with a validation error rather than downscaled, so the application must resize before returning them and scale coordinates back afterwards","B. `tool_result` blocks cannot contain image content at all","C. Screenshots must be JPEG; PNG is rejected inside tool results","D. `max_tokens` must be raised to accommodate the screenshot's visual tokens"],a:0,
e:"Screenshots and zoom images returned to the **computer use and browser use toolsets are the exception to automatic resizing**: the API rejects an oversized `tool_result` image with a validation error instead of silently scaling it. That is deliberate, because these are coordinate-bearing images and a silent rescale would misdirect every click. Resize in your application to the size the model processes, feed that to the model, then scale the coordinates Claude returns back to the real screen, dividing by the display scale factor on HiDPI screens.",
w:"B — `tool_result.content` may be an array of content blocks including images; that is how screenshots reach the model. C — both PNG and JPEG are supported; format is not the trigger. D — `max_tokens` caps generated output and has no bearing on input image validation."},

{id:1245,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Can a tool return an image to Claude, and if so how?",
o:["A. No; tool results must be plain text or a JSON string","B. Yes; `tool_result.content` may be an array of content blocks, including `image` blocks","C. Yes, but only by following the tool result with a separate `user` message carrying the image","D. Only the built-in computer use tool may return images; custom tools cannot"],a:1,
e:"Tool results are **`tool_result` blocks inside a `user` message**, matched to the call by `tool_use_id`, and their `content` may be an **array of content blocks including images**. That makes genuinely multimodal tools possible: a chart renderer, a map tile fetcher, a document page extractor. Two consequences follow. Images nested in tool results are billed as input visual tokens like any other image, and they count toward the request's image limits, including the stricter per-image dimension rule that applies once a request carries more than 20 image and document blocks.",
w:"A — restricting results to text is the common misconception; structured content blocks are supported. C — a trailing user message would break the `tool_use_id` pairing that the API requires and is unnecessary. D — nothing about image-bearing results is special to the built-in toolsets."},

{id:1246,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A design-review assistant accumulates images across a long conversation. On top of resending the accumulated history each turn, the client also appends copies of every earlier image into the newest `user` turn \"so Claude remembers them\". Input token spend climbs far faster than expected. What is the assessment?",
o:["A. Correct and required, because the API is stateless and images must be restated in the current turn","B. Correct for `base64` sources but unnecessary for `file_id` sources","C. Unnecessary duplication: the resent history already contains those image blocks, and Claude can reference every image from earlier turns, so the copies double the visual-token bill for nothing","D. Correct, but the duplicates should be submitted through the Batch API to halve their cost"],a:2,
e:"Statelessness means you resend the accumulated **history**, not that you must restate its contents inside the newest turn. Claude has access to every image already present in that history, so a follow-up like \"is this similar to the first two?\" works without re-attaching them. Appending copies simply pays for the same patches twice. The two real optimisations are referencing images by **Files API `file_id`** so each turn carries an identifier rather than bytes, and caching the stable image prefix with `cache_control` so repeated turns read it at about 0.1x.",
w:"A — statelessness explains resending history; it does not justify duplicating blocks that history already contains. B — the duplication wastes visual tokens with either source type, since billing is on patches, not on bytes transmitted. D — the Batch API is for asynchronous bulk jobs, not interactive turns, and discounting waste is not the same as removing it."},

{id:1247,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agentic workflow attaches up to eight base64 screenshots per turn and runs for dozens of turns. Around turn 20 requests begin failing with `413 request_too_large`. What is the correct remedy?",
o:["A. Compress the screenshots more aggressively as JPEG until the payload fits","B. Lower `max_tokens` so the request is smaller","C. Summarise older turns so there are fewer turns to resend","D. Upload each image once to the Files API and reference it by `file_id`, so each turn transmits identifiers rather than the full image bytes"],a:3,
e:"Because the API is stateless, every turn resends the whole history, and with `base64` sources that means **retransmitting the full bytes of every accumulated image on every turn**. Request payload grows without bound and eventually crosses the request size limit, returning **`413 request_too_large`**. The Files API breaks that coupling: upload once, then reference by **`file_id`**, and each turn carries a short identifier regardless of how many images have accumulated. Note this is a payload fix, not a billing fix; the images are still priced as visual tokens each turn.",
w:"A — heavier lossy compression buys a little headroom while introducing artefacts that damage exactly the small text screenshots are read for. B — `max_tokens` bounds generated output and does not shrink the request body. C — trimming history helps context pressure, but it discards conversation the agent may need, and the payload problem returns as soon as new images accumulate."},

{id:1248,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A working first-party image pipeline uses the Files API and references images by `file_id`. The team must redeploy it against Amazon Bedrock. What has to change?",
o:["A. Rework the image blocks to `base64` sources, because only base64 image sources are available on Amazon Bedrock and Google Cloud, and account for the lower 5 MB per-image ceiling there","B. Nothing; `file_id` references are portable across all platforms Claude is offered on","C. Switch to `url` sources, which is the supported alternative on Bedrock","D. Replace image blocks with `document` blocks, the only multimodal input Bedrock accepts"],a:0,
e:"On **Amazon Bedrock and Google Cloud, only `base64` image sources are available**; neither `url` nor `file` sources are offered. Any pipeline built on `file_id` must inline bytes again, which reinstates the payload growth that the Files API was solving. Two further numbers move: the per-image ceiling is **5 MB base64 on those platforms versus 10 MB on the Claude API**, and overall request size limits are lower. Plan a downsampling and chunking strategy as part of the port rather than discovering it under load.",
w:"B — portability is the trap; source-type availability is exactly what differs between first-party and partner-operated platforms. C — `url` sources are unavailable there too, so this substitutes one unsupported source for another. D — `document` blocks are for PDFs and text, and image blocks are supported on Bedrock in base64 form."},

{id:1249,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What is the maximum size of a single image?",
o:["A. 32 MB on every platform, matching the request size limit","B. 10 MB base64-encoded on the Claude API, and 5 MB on Amazon Bedrock and Google Cloud","C. 5 MB base64-encoded everywhere","D. 20 MB on the Claude API and 10 MB on partner clouds"],a:1,
e:"Per-image limits are **10 MB base64-encoded on the Claude API** and **5 MB on Amazon Bedrock and Google Cloud**. These are distinct from the request-level size limit (32 MB for standard endpoints, lower on some partner platforms) and from the dimension rules: 8000 px maximum on the longest side, plus a stricter per-image dimension limit once a request carries more than 20 image and document blocks. A high-volume pipeline needs all three checks, because each produces a different failure and only some of them are recoverable by resizing.",
w:"A — 32 MB is the request-level size limit for the whole payload, not a per-image allowance. C — 5 MB is the partner-cloud figure; the Claude API allows twice that. D — neither number is correct, and the direction of the difference between first-party and partner platforms is reversed."},

{id:1250,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"How many images may a single request contain?",
o:["A. 20 per request on the API, matching the claude.ai limit","B. 100 per request on every model","C. 100 per request on models with a 200k-token context window and 600 on other models, with 20 per turn on claude.ai","D. Unlimited, bounded only by the 32 MB request size limit"],a:2,
e:"The API allows **100 images per request on models with a 200k-token context window and 600 on other models**, while claude.ai allows **20 per message**. In practice the request size limit is often reached first, which is why the Files API is recommended for many images. Two further constraints bite before the ceiling: crossing **20 image and document blocks** in one request imposes a stricter per-image dimension limit on every image in it, and requests carrying many large images can fail even under the count limit and even via `file_id`.",
w:"A — 20 is the claude.ai per-message limit; the API allows far more. B — 100 applies to small-context models; larger-context models allow 600. D — there is a hard count limit as well as the size limit, and both can be reached."},

{id:1251,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A batch job sends 45 page images of 2400x1700 px each in one request. It is rejected with an `invalid_request_error` whose message refers to \"many-image requests\" and quotes a limit in pixels. Individually each image is well within the normal limits. What is the rule?",
o:["A. 45 images exceeds the API's 20-image ceiling","B. The total payload exceeded the 32 MB request size limit","C. At least one image exceeded the 10 MB per-image size limit","D. Once a request contains more than 20 image and document blocks, a stricter per-image dimension limit applies to every image in it; resize so neither dimension exceeds 2000 px, or keep the request to 20 or fewer blocks"],a:3,
e:"Beyond the ordinary count, size, and tier limits there is a **threshold rule**: a request with more than 20 image and document blocks applies a **stricter per-image dimension limit to every image in that request**, and images exceeding it are rejected rather than downscaled. The portable remedies are to keep each image within 2000 px on both dimensions, or to split the work so any single request carries 20 or fewer blocks. Note this is a dimension rule, so compressing the files harder changes nothing.",
w:"A — the API ceiling is 100 or 600 images depending on the model's context window, not 20; 20 is the threshold that tightens dimensions. B — a size overflow returns a request-too-large style failure and would not reference many-image dimension limits. C — a 2400x1700 image is nowhere near 10 MB, and the error names a pixel limit rather than a byte limit."},

{id:1252,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A computer-use agent works fine for its first dozen steps, then every request starts failing with a many-image dimension error, even though each turn adds only one screenshot. What explains the sudden onset?",
o:["A. Every `image` block in the request counts toward the 20-block threshold, including images from earlier turns that you resend and images nested inside `tool_result` content, so an accumulating screenshot history eventually crosses it","B. Only images in the newest `user` turn count toward the threshold, so something else changed","C. Images nested in `tool_result` blocks are exempt from the threshold","D. The threshold is evaluated per turn rather than per request, so history is irrelevant"],a:0,
e:"The threshold is evaluated over the **whole request**, and **all** image blocks count: freshly attached images, images from earlier turns that statelessness obliges you to resend, and images nested inside `tool_result` content such as computer-use screenshots. On Amazon Bedrock and Google Cloud, `document` blocks count too. So a loop that adds one screenshot per step crosses 20 blocks around step 20 and every image in the request is suddenly held to the stricter dimension limit. Cap screenshot history, downsample retained frames, or keep each within 2000 px from the start.",
w:"B — the threshold counts the request, and resent history is part of the request under a stateless API. C — tool-result images are explicitly included in the count. D — there is no per-turn evaluation; the accumulated request is what is measured."},

{id:1253,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A document service uploads 300 page images to the Files API and references them all by `file_id` in one request, comfortably under the 600-image ceiling and with a tiny request body. The request still fails. What is the explanation?",
o:["A. The 600-image ceiling applies per conversation rather than per request, and earlier turns consumed it","B. Even with the Files API, requests with many large images can fail before reaching the image-count ceiling; reduce dimensions or file sizes before uploading, or split the job across requests","C. Images referenced by `file_id` do not count toward the image limit, so the failure must be unrelated to images","D. `max_tokens` must be raised to accommodate 300 pages of visual tokens"],a:1,
e:"`file_id` references keep the **request payload** small, but they do not shrink what the model must process. Requests with many large images can fail before the 600-image count, so the documented guidance is to **downsample dimensions and file sizes before uploading** and to split large jobs. Do the arithmetic: 300 pages at even 1500 visual tokens each is 450,000 tokens of input before any text. Chunk by pages per request, then aggregate results in your own code.",
w:"A — the ceiling is per request; a stateless API has no server-side conversation to accumulate against. C — `file_id` images count exactly like any other image; only the transport differs. D — `max_tokens` bounds generated output and cannot relieve input-side pressure."},

{id:1254,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What is the recommended ordering of image and text content blocks within a user turn?",
o:["A. Text first in all cases, so the instruction frames the image","B. Ordering has no measurable effect on quality","C. Prefer image-then-text; images placed after or interleaved with text still perform well, but image-first performs best","D. Images must be the only content in their own turn, with the question sent as a separate turn"],a:2,
e:"The guidance mirrors long-context prompting, where documents go before the query: **put images before text**. Images placed after text or interleaved with it still perform well, so this is a preference rather than a constraint, but where your use case allows it, image-then-text is the better default. The same advice applies to PDFs: place `document` blocks before the prompt. When several images are involved, the pattern combines both rules: a short text label, then the image, repeated, then the question last.",
w:"A — text-first is the intuitive but weaker default; the recommendation runs the other way. B — the effect is documented as real, which is why the guidance exists at all. D — a single turn may freely mix image and text blocks, and splitting them into separate turns adds nothing."},

{id:1255,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A floor-plan reviewer sends three unlabelled images followed by the question, then asks in a follow-up turn \"what changed between the second and the third?\". Claude's answer conflates the plans. What is the fix?",
o:["A. Send each image in its own request and stitch the answers together in your code","B. Ask Claude to number the images itself in its first response, then refer to those numbers","C. Reference the images by `file_id` so their original filenames identify them","D. Introduce each image with a short adjacent text block such as `Image 1:`, so the prompt and every later turn can refer to them unambiguously"],a:3,
e:"With multiple images, **labelling them with adjacent text blocks** is what makes reference reliable: `Image 1:`, image, `Image 2:`, image, then the question. The labels become shared vocabulary that survives into follow-up turns, where positional phrases like \"the second one\" are exactly what goes wrong once history grows. This costs a handful of text tokens and removes an entire class of misattribution, which is the sort of trade every multi-image pipeline should take.",
w:"A — separate requests destroy the joint comparison that the model is being asked to perform. B — model-assigned numbering is not stable across turns and adds a round trip for something you can state deterministically. C — Claude never sees filenames or any image metadata, so a `file_id` is not a label."},

{id:1256,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An insurance intake tool asks Claude to report each photo's capture timestamp and GPS location from its EXIF data, to verify when a loss occurred. Claude either declines or invents plausible values. What is the correct design?",
o:["A. Claude receives no image metadata at all; parse EXIF in your own code and pass the timestamp and coordinates as text alongside the image","B. Instruct Claude in the system prompt to read the metadata block before describing the photo","C. Upload via the Files API, which forwards file metadata to the model","D. Send the photo as a `document` block, which preserves embedded metadata"],a:0,
e:"**Claude does not parse or receive any metadata from images.** Only pixels reach the model, so EXIF timestamps, GPS tags, camera model, and filenames are invisible, and asking for them invites confabulation. Extract EXIF deterministically in your own pipeline and inject the fields as text next to the image. The transferable principle: anything that must be exact and is already available as structured data should be read by code and handed to the model, never inferred by it. For evidential use, also verify the metadata rather than trusting the uploader.",
w:"B — no instruction can surface data that was never delivered to the model. C — the Files API is transport and storage; it does not add a metadata channel. D — `document` blocks are for PDFs and plain text and confer no metadata visibility."},

{id:1257,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A privacy review asks what happens to images sent inline in a Messages request, and how to delete them afterwards.",
o:["A. They are stored for 30 days and can be removed with a delete call","B. Image uploads are ephemeral and are deleted after processing, so there is nothing to delete; use the Files API when you need a durable, referenceable copy","C. They are retained for model training unless the organisation opts out","D. They persist server-side for the lifetime of the conversation so later turns can reference them"],a:1,
e:"Images passed inline are **ephemeral: not stored beyond the duration of the API request, and automatically deleted once processed**. There is consequently no image-deletion endpoint, and uploaded images are not used to train models. This is also why later turns must resend the history containing the image: the API is stateless and nothing is held for you. If you do need a persistent, reusable handle, that is the Files API's job, and its lifecycle is managed explicitly rather than implicitly.",
w:"A — no retention window or deletion call exists for inline images. C — uploaded images are not used for training. D — nothing persists server-side between requests; the stateless model is what forces history to be resent."},

{id:1258,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A marketplace trust team wants Claude to flag listing photos that were generated by an image model. In a pilot Claude answers confidently and is frequently wrong in both directions. What should the team conclude?",
o:["A. Switch to a high-resolution-tier model so generation artefacts become visible","B. Constrain the answer with a structured-output schema carrying a confidence score","C. Claude cannot determine whether an image is AI-generated and may be confidently incorrect; this needs a purpose-built detector plus human review, with Claude used only for adjacent signals such as description and policy checks","D. Send each photo several times and take a majority vote across the responses"],a:2,
e:"This is a **documented capability boundary, not a tuning problem**: Claude cannot determine whether an image is AI-generated and should not be relied on to detect synthetic images. No resolution tier, schema, or sampling trick converts an absent capability into a reliable one. The transferable principle is to map requirements onto documented capabilities before designing: use a dedicated provenance or detection system for the decision, keep humans on the enforcement path, and give Claude the work it is good at, such as describing a listing or checking it against written policy.",
w:"A — extra pixels do not create a discrimination ability the model does not have. B — structured outputs guarantee the shape of a response, and a self-reported confidence number from an unreliable judgement is itself unreliable. D — repeated sampling smooths variance around a wrong central tendency and does not fix a systematic capability gap."},

{id:1259,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A physical-security product wants Claude to look at camera stills and name which employee appears, matching against a supplied staff roster. What should the team be told?",
o:["A. It works once a system prompt states that the organisation consents to identification","B. It works if each still is accompanied by labelled reference photos of the candidates","C. It works on high-resolution-tier models, which resolve faces well enough","D. Claude cannot be used to name people in images and will refuse; identity matching must happen outside the model, with Claude limited to non-identifying tasks"],a:3,
e:"Naming people in images is **prohibited by the Acceptable Use Policy, and Claude refuses**. This is a policy boundary, so it does not yield to prompting, reference images, or a better model. Architecturally, keep identity resolution in a purpose-built system with its own consent, retention, and audit controls, and scope Claude to non-identifying work such as counting occupants, describing clothing, or flagging whether a door is open. The transferable principle: enforce policy in the surrounding system rather than trying to negotiate it inside the prompt.",
w:"A — the model is not a permissions system, and an asserted consent in a prompt does not lift an AUP restriction. B — supplying reference photos is still identification and is refused for the same reason. C — resolution is irrelevant to a policy limit."},

{id:1260,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A catalogue tool feeds Claude 120x90 px thumbnails and asks for the brand printed on each product. Readings are frequently invented. What is the most effective change?",
o:["A. Send the full-resolution crop rather than the thumbnail; Claude is unreliable on very small images, under roughly 200 px, and may hallucinate detail","B. Upscale each thumbnail four times with a super-resolution filter before sending","C. Send more thumbnails per request so Claude can compare them and self-correct","D. Move to a cheaper model tier, since small images need little capability"],a:0,
e:"Accuracy degrades on **low-quality, rotated, or very small images, specifically those under about 200 pixels**, and the failure mode is confident invention rather than refusal. A 120x90 thumbnail costs only `⌈120/28⌉ x ⌈90/28⌉` = 5 x 4 = 20 visual tokens, so there is almost nothing to reason from. Send the original crop at a legible size. The transferable principle: when text in an image matters, spend visual tokens on that text, cropping to the region of interest rather than shrinking the whole frame.",
w:"B — upscaling adds pixels but no information, and interpolation artefacts can make invented readings more confident, not less. C — more unreadable inputs do not average into a readable one. D — a smaller model makes a hard perception task harder; the input, not the model, is the bottleneck."},

{id:1261,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A clinical startup proposes sending CT slices to Claude for diagnostic impressions that clinicians would sign off. What is the correct position?",
o:["A. Acceptable with a specialist system prompt describing the reporting conventions","B. Claude can analyse general medical images but is not designed to interpret complex diagnostic scans such as CT or MRI, and its output is not a substitute for professional diagnosis; the diagnostic decision cannot rest on it","C. Acceptable if each slice is sent as a separately labelled image so the series is reconstructed","D. Acceptable on high-resolution-tier models, whose visual-token budget resolves fine anatomy"],a:1,
e:"The documented limitation is explicit: Claude can analyse general medical images but **is not designed to interpret complex diagnostic scans such as CT or MRI**, and outputs must not be treated as professional medical advice or diagnosis. High-stakes vision work needs human oversight regardless of tier, and this class of work needs a regulated, purpose-built system. Reframe the product around defensible tasks such as drafting report structure, checking a report against a protocol, or extracting fields from a written radiology report.",
w:"A — a prompt cannot confer a clinical capability or discharge a regulatory obligation. C — slice labelling improves reference, not diagnostic validity, and the limitation is about the task rather than the packaging. D — resolution tier changes token cost and detail retention, not the appropriateness of the use case."},

{id:1262,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A retail audit tool asks Claude to count facings on shelf photos. Counts are close but drift by a few units, and the drift is worst on densely packed shelves. What is the sound engineering response?",
o:["A. Raise the image resolution above the tier limit so more detail survives","B. Send each shelf twice and reconcile the two answers","C. Treat the figure as an estimate: Claude gives approximate counts and is least precise with large numbers of small objects, so verify with a deterministic detector or a human check where the number is consequential","D. Request the count as an integer through a structured-output schema, which makes the value accurate"],a:2,
e:"**Counting is a documented approximation**, degrading with many small objects, so the design question is not how to make the model exact but where the exactness must live. Use Claude for triage, description, and anomaly flagging, and put the authoritative count in a deterministic detector or a human spot check with a defined tolerance. The transferable principle: choose the component that owns a guarantee, and never let a probabilistic component own a number that a business process treats as exact.",
w:"A — images above the tier limits are downscaled anyway, so the extra pixels are discarded before the model sees them. B — two samples of an approximate process give you two approximations and no ground truth. D — structured outputs guarantee that the response matches the schema, never that the value inside it is correct."},

{id:1263,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"To cut request size, an ingestion service re-encodes every uploaded receipt as JPEG at low quality, and some images pass through two compression stages. Latency improves; field-level OCR accuracy falls sharply on small print. What is the explanation?",
o:["A. WebP is the only lossy format supported, so the JPEG path silently degraded","B. Re-encoding changed the `media_type`, so the image was misinterpreted","C. Compression reduced the image below the roughly 200 px threshold at which accuracy degrades","D. Lossy compression, particularly repeated passes, introduces artefacts that make small text hard to read; inspect the actual bytes you send and back the quality setting off"],a:3,
e:"Compressing with a lossy format does reduce request size and latency, and it is recommended for that reason, but **artefacts are detrimental to model performance, especially when multiple compression passes are applied**, and heavy JPEG compression makes text difficult to read. Note that compression does not reduce visual-token cost at all: patches are computed from dimensions, not bytes. So a quality-destroying setting buys bandwidth only. Verify by inspecting the exact images sent to the API, not the originals.",
w:"A — both JPEG and WebP are supported, and JPEG is explicitly suggested for size reduction. B — the `media_type` is whatever you declare, and re-encoding to JPEG with `image/jpeg` is consistent. C — quality-based compression does not change pixel dimensions, so the small-image threshold is untouched."},

{id:1264,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An engineering-drawing reader sends 3200x2400 px sheets to a standard-tier model. Large labels are read correctly, but small dimension callouts are frequently wrong. What addresses this best?",
o:["A. Instruct Claude to zoom in on the callout regions before reading them","B. Crop the region of interest and send the crop, offsetting any returned coordinates by the crop origin, or move to a high-resolution-tier model","C. Send the sheet twice, once at full size and once downscaled, and ask Claude to reconcile the two readings","D. Raise `max_tokens` so there is room to report every callout"],a:1,
e:"A 3200x2400 sheet costs `115 x 86` = 9890 patches, far above the standard tier's 1568-token budget, so it is downscaled hard and **small elements lose the pixels that made them legible**. The two documented remedies are to **crop the region of interest and send the crop**, which spends the whole token budget on the part you care about, or to use a **high-resolution-tier model** with its larger 4784-token budget. If you crop, remember to add the crop origin back to any coordinates Claude returns.",
w:"A — Claude cannot zoom; it sees one fixed rasterisation of what you send, so zooming is your job. C — the full-size copy is downscaled to roughly the same thing as the manual one, so you pay twice for the same information. D — `max_tokens` bounds the response length and cannot recover input detail that was discarded before inference."},

{id:1265,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"Of a model's two image limits, which one determines the final processed size for most real inputs?",
o:["A. The edge limit almost always binds first, so scaling the long edge to the limit is a safe approximation","B. Whichever limit the client selects in the image block","C. For nearly all photos and screenshots the visual-token limit determines the final size; the edge limit takes over only for elongated images such as panoramas and very tall screenshots","D. Both bind simultaneously, because each tier's edge and token numbers are matched by design"],a:2,
e:"Claude finds the largest aspect-preserving size satisfying both the edge limit and the visual-token budget, and for typical aspect ratios it is the **token budget that binds**. The edge limit only takes over for elongated shapes, where a long side breaks the edge cap while the total patch count stays modest. This is why 1920x1080 resizes to 1456x819 rather than 1568x882: assuming the edge limit puts every coordinate noticeably off target. Compute the size with the reference implementation instead of scaling by hand.",
w:"A — this is the standard mistake and it produces wrong dimensions for ordinary screenshots. B — the limits are properties of the model's resolution tier and are not client-selectable. D — the standard tier's numbers being equal is a coincidence of units, not simultaneity; pixels and patch counts scale differently."},

{id:1266,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team pre-resizes 1920x1080 screenshots to 1568x882 by scaling the long edge to the standard-tier limit, then uses the returned click points directly. Points land consistently low and to the right. What is wrong?",
o:["A. Claude returned normalised coordinates that still need multiplying by the screen size","B. Bottom-right padding shifted the coordinate origin","C. The model was actually on the high-resolution tier, which does not resize at all","D. The visual-token budget, not the edge limit, sets the final size: 1920x1080 resizes to 1456x819 on the standard tier, so the image was resized again after their manual scaling and every coordinate refers to the smaller size"],a:3,
e:"1568x882 costs `56 x 32` = 1792 patches, still above the 1568-token budget, so the server resizes again, to **1456x819**. The team's coordinates therefore refer to an image the model never saw. Scaling the long edge to the edge limit is the classic error: the correct target satisfies **both** limits, and for ordinary aspect ratios the token budget is the binding one. Use the published resize helper to compute the exact target, resize to that, and do not pad yourself.",
w:"A — Claude returns absolute pixel coordinates in the image it processed, not normalised values. B — padding is bottom-and-right only, so the origin does not move; padding causes error only if you normalise by padded dimensions. C — a high-resolution-tier model would leave 1920x1080 untouched, which would make the coordinates correct rather than skewed."},

{id:1267,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A cost model for an image-heavy product needs an upper bound on the input visual tokens any single image can contribute. What sets that bound?",
o:["A. The tier's visual-token budget: any image exceeding the limits is downscaled to fit, so per-image visual cost cannot exceed about 1568 tokens on the standard tier or 4784 on the high-resolution tier","B. Nothing bounds it; visual cost grows with the original pixel count","C. `max_tokens`, which caps the tokens attributable to the request","D. The 10 MB per-image size limit, converted to tokens at the model's bytes-per-token rate"],a:0,
e:"Because oversized images are **downscaled to the largest size fitting both tier limits**, the visual-token budget doubles as a hard per-image ceiling: about **1568 tokens standard tier, 4784 high-resolution tier**. That makes worst-case cost modelling tractable: multiply the tier ceiling by images per request and by request volume for an upper bound, then refine with the patch formula for your real dimensions. It also explains why a tier change is a cost event, since the ceiling roughly triples on the high-resolution tier.",
w:"B — unbounded growth is the intuition the downscaling rule exists to prevent. C — `max_tokens` caps generated output only and never appears in input accounting. D — bytes-per-token conversion applies to text; image cost is patch-based and independent of file size."},

{id:1268,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An engineer argues that sending 4K originals instead of pre-resizing them \"wastes money on pixels Claude throws away\". Is that right, and what does oversizing actually cost?",
o:["A. Yes, roughly eight times the visual tokens, in proportion to the discarded pixel area","B. No: oversized images are downscaled server-side to the same size your own resize would produce, so visual-token cost is identical; what you pay extra is request payload size, upload latency, and the risk that small text becomes illegible after a resize you did not control","C. No, and there is no downside of any kind to sending originals","D. Yes, and the request is additionally rejected once the long edge passes 1568 px"],a:1,
e:"Downscaling **caps** visual-token cost, so the token bill for a 4K page and for its correctly pre-resized equivalent is the same. The real penalties are elsewhere: larger request bodies that push toward the size limit and `413`, higher upload latency, and loss of control over legibility, because a server-side resize you did not compute may destroy the small text you needed. Pre-resizing also keeps coordinate workflows honest, since the image you hold is the image Claude saw. Reserve genuinely large inputs for the high-resolution tier, where the extra fidelity is actually retained.",
w:"A — token cost is capped by the tier budget, so discarded pixels are not billed. C — the claim of no downside ignores payload size, latency, and uncontrolled legibility loss. D — exceeding the edge limit triggers downscaling, not rejection, unless the image is marked to error or breaks the 8000 px hard ceiling."},

{id:1269,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"What are the documented limits for native PDF processing in a single Messages request?",
o:["A. 10 MB and 100 pages on every model","B. 32 MB and 100 pages on every model","C. A 32 MB maximum request size and 600 pages per request, dropping to 100 pages when the request's context window is under 1M tokens","D. No page limit; only the 32 MB request size applies"],a:2,
e:"Native PDF support allows a **32 MB maximum request size** and **600 pages per request, or 100 when the request's context window is under 1M tokens**. The PDF must be a standard file with no password or encryption. Both limits apply to the **entire request payload**, not just the document block, so a large system prompt or accompanying images eat into the same budget. For large files, upload via the Files API and reference by `file_id` to keep the request body small, and split dense documents into sections.",
w:"A — 10 MB is the per-image size limit, not the PDF request size, and 100 pages is the small-context figure rather than a universal one. B — the page figure depends on the request's context window; 600 applies at 1M and above. D — there is a hard page limit as well as a size limit."},

{id:1270,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A contract-review request bundles a 28 MB PDF, a long retrieved-clause library in the system prompt, and two exhibit images. It fails on size even though the PDF alone is under 32 MB. Why?",
o:["A. Only the `document` block counts toward 32 MB, so something else must have failed","B. The system prompt is excluded from request size accounting","C. Attachments are measured separately from the JSON body","D. Both PDF limits apply to the entire request payload, including everything sent alongside the document, so the PDF plus the clause library plus the images crossed 32 MB together"],a:3,
e:"The **32 MB size limit and the page limit are both properties of the whole request payload**, not of the document block in isolation. A 28 MB PDF leaves under 4 MB for base64 images, system prompt, and history, and base64 encoding inflates binary content by roughly a third. Two fixes compose well: move the PDF to the Files API and reference it by `file_id` so its bytes leave the body, and split the document into sections so each request carries a manageable slice.",
w:"A — the limit is payload-wide, which is exactly the trap in this scenario. B — the system prompt is part of the request body and counts in full. C — there is no separate attachment channel; everything is one JSON payload."},

{id:1271,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"E",
q:"A client portal receives password-protected PDFs from law firms. Every such document fails when sent to Claude, while unprotected files from the same firms succeed. What is required?",
o:["A. Claude requires a standard PDF with no password or encryption; remove the protection in your own pipeline, with the appropriate authorisation, before sending","B. Pass the password in a field on the `document` block","C. Base64-encode the file, which strips the encryption layer","D. Use a `url` source, which handles encrypted documents"],a:0,
e:"The documented requirement is a **standard PDF with no passwords or encryption**. There is no field for supplying a password, so decryption must happen upstream in code that is authorised to do it, ideally with the event logged, since you are handling protected client material. Build this as an explicit validation step at ingest: detect encryption, either decrypt with a held credential or route the document to a human, and fail loudly rather than letting an opaque API error surface to the user.",
w:"B — no password parameter exists on the `document` block. C — base64 is an encoding of the same encrypted bytes and changes nothing about readability. D — the source type only determines how the file reaches the API, not whether it can be parsed."},

{id:1272,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"How does native PDF support process each page of a document?",
o:["A. Text is extracted only, so charts, diagrams, and layout are invisible","B. Each page is converted to an image and its text is extracted, and Claude receives both together","C. Pages are converted to images only, so all reading is visual","D. The raw PDF bytes are passed through and parsed by the model itself"],a:1,
e:"The pipeline **converts each page into an image and extracts that page's text, supplying both to the model**. That dual representation is why you can ask about charts, diagrams, tables, and layout as well as wording, and it is also why cost has two components: roughly 1,500 to 3,000 text tokens per page depending on density, plus image tokens for each rendered page. It also explains the main trade-off against rasterising pages yourself: doing it manually gives you control over page dimensions but loses the extracted text layer.",
w:"A — text-only extraction is the behaviour of the legacy Bedrock Converse mode without citations, not of native PDF support. C — an image-only pipeline is what you get when you rasterise pages yourself; native support adds the text layer. D — the model does not parse container formats; the platform performs the extraction."},

{id:1273,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"A team is deciding between native `document` blocks and converting PDF pages to images themselves. What do they give up by rasterising pages in their own code?",
o:["A. Nothing; the two paths are equivalent in what the model receives","B. Prompt caching, which only applies to `document` blocks","C. The extracted text layer that native PDF support supplies alongside each page image, so the model must read everything visually; weigh that against the control over page dimensions that manual rasterisation gains","D. Access to the Files API, which only accepts PDFs"],a:2,
e:"Native support gives the model **image plus extracted text per page**. Rasterise yourself and you send images only, so every character is read visually: more fragile on small type, and usually more visual tokens for the same page. What you gain is **control of the rasterisation dimensions**, which native support chooses server-side, and that control is mandatory for coordinate and bounding-box workflows. Choose per requirement: native for comprehension and simplicity, manual rasterisation when geometry or per-page pre-processing is load-bearing.",
w:"A — the text layer is a real difference in what reaches the model, with measurable accuracy and cost consequences. B — prompt caching works on image blocks as well; the cacheable prefix rule is about ordering and byte-identity, not block type. D — the Files API accepts images and plain text as well as PDFs."},

{id:1274,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"How should the token cost of a 40-page PDF be estimated?",
o:["A. Count only the extracted text; the page images are supplied for free","B. Apply a flat per-document PDF processing surcharge on top of the text tokens","C. Count only the page images, since text is read from them","D. Count both: roughly 1,500 to 3,000 text tokens per page depending on content density, plus image tokens for each page's rendered image, at standard pricing with no additional PDF fee"],a:3,
e:"Because every page is delivered as **text plus an image**, cost has two components: about **1,500 to 3,000 text tokens per page** depending on density, plus the **image tokens** for the rendered page computed with the same `⌈w/28⌉ x ⌈h/28⌉` patch rule as any image. Standard pricing applies and there is no PDF surcharge. Use `/v1/messages/count_tokens`, which is free and returns a single `input_tokens` estimate, to price a representative document before committing to a volume.",
w:"A — page images are billed as visual tokens like any other image. B — no per-document fee exists; PDFs are priced purely as tokens. C — the extracted text is a real and often dominant part of the bill."},

{id:1275,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A 250-page technical manual with dense small-font pages, large tables, and heavy graphics fails on context limits, even though 250 is well under the documented page ceiling. What is happening and what is the fix?",
o:["A. Dense pages can exhaust the context window long before the page-count limit; split the document into sections, and downsample embedded images since each page is processed as an image","B. The 600-page limit is a daily account-level allowance that had been consumed","C. Page limits only apply to `base64` sources, so switching to the Files API resolves it","D. Raise `max_tokens` so the model has room to work through 250 pages"],a:0,
e:"The page ceiling and the context window are **independent constraints**, and for dense documents the context window binds first: at 1,500 to 3,000 text tokens plus image tokens per page, 250 pages can run to several hundred thousand tokens. The documented remedies are to **split the document into sections** and to **downsample embedded images**, since each page is processed as an image. Note the context window bounds the request plus all generated output including thinking, so a big document also squeezes the answer.",
w:"B — the page limit is per request, not a daily allowance. C — the Files API reduces payload size, not the tokens the model must process, so the context problem survives the switch. D — `max_tokens` adds to the output side of the same window and makes the pressure worse, not better."},

{id:1276,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A diligence tool asks twelve different questions about the same 90-page contract, one request at a time, within a few minutes. Input spend is twelve times the document cost. What is the right optimisation?",
o:["A. Send the contract once and rely on server-side conversation memory for the remaining questions","B. Place the `document` block first and mark it with `cache_control`, so the first request pays about 1.25x to write the prefix and the remaining eleven read it at about 0.1x","C. Split the contract into twelve eight-page slices, one per question","D. Submit the twelve questions through the Batch API to halve the cost"],a:1,
e:"A large, unchanging document read repeatedly is the textbook **prompt caching** case. Put the document at the front of the prefix, place a `cache_control: {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}` breakpoint on the block that ends the cacheable region, and keep the bytes byte-identical across requests. The write costs about **1.25x** base input, each read about **0.1x**, and the default TTL is **5 minutes, refreshed on every read**, so a burst of twelve questions stays warm. Remember the prefix order is tools, then system, then messages.",
w:"A — the API is stateless; there is no server-side memory, so history must be resent on every call. C — slicing loses cross-document reasoning and still pays full price for each slice. D — the Batch API discount is roughly 50% but results arrive asynchronously within 24 hours, which does not suit an interactive tool, and caching yields a far larger saving here."},

{id:1277,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A pipeline caches a prefix containing eight reference images. `usage` shows `cache_creation_input_tokens` on nearly every request and almost no `cache_read_input_tokens`. The images are logically the same each run, but the pipeline re-reads and re-encodes them from source each time. What is the cause?",
o:["A. Image blocks cannot participate in prompt caching","B. The cache TTL for image content is only 60 seconds","C. A cached prefix must be byte-identical; re-encoding produces different base64 on each run, so nothing matches and every request pays the 1.25x write instead of the 0.1x read. Encode once and reuse the exact bytes, or reference a stable `file_id`","D. Caching only applies when the image blocks come last in the message"],a:2,
e:"Cache matching is on **exact bytes of the prefix**, so any re-encoding, changed compression setting, or re-serialisation invalidates it, even when the picture is visually identical. The symptom is diagnostic: high `cache_creation_input_tokens` with negligible `cache_read_input_tokens` means you are paying the **1.25x write** repeatedly and never collecting the **0.1x read**. Fix it by materialising the encoded bytes once and reusing them verbatim, or by uploading to the Files API and referencing a stable `file_id`. Also remember there are at most 4 breakpoints; a 5th returns 400.",
w:"A — images cache like any other content; byte-identity is the requirement. B — the default TTL is 5 minutes, refreshed on each read, with a 1-hour option, and it is not content-type specific. D — the cacheable region is a prefix, so a breakpoint must come after the content you want cached, not before the end of the message."},

{id:1278,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"In what order is the cacheable prefix assembled, and what does that mean for an image in the first user message?",
o:["A. messages, then system, then tools","B. system, then messages, then tools","C. Order is unspecified; each breakpoint caches only its own block","D. tools, then system, then messages, so a `cache_control` breakpoint on an image block in the first user message also covers the tool definitions and system content that precede it"],a:3,
e:"The cacheable prefix is ordered **tools, then system, then messages**. A breakpoint marks the *end* of a cacheable prefix, so putting one on an image block early in the first user message captures everything before it too: tool definitions, the system prompt, and any preceding message content. That is usually what you want for a document or reference-image workload. Two caveats: everything before the breakpoint must stay byte-identical, and changing `tool_choice` invalidates cached **message** blocks while tools and system stay cached.",
w:"A — the order is reversed, which would imply messages could be cached independently of the system prompt preceding them. B — system does not precede tools in the prefix. C — breakpoints define prefixes rather than isolated blocks, which is precisely why ordering matters."},

{id:1279,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A 30,000-request batch classifies scanned forms against a shared cached instruction-and-exemplar prefix. `usage` across the results shows cache reads on only the earliest requests. What should change?",
o:["A. Use the 1-hour cache TTL, because the 5-minute default expires while a batch that may take up to 24 hours is still being worked through","B. Prompt caching does not apply inside batches, so the early reads were a reporting artefact","C. Submit smaller batches of about 500 requests each so every batch finishes inside the TTL","D. Raise the number of cache breakpoints to five so more of the prefix stays resident"],a:0,
e:"Batches complete **within 24 hours**, while the default cache TTL is **5 minutes, refreshed on each read**. On a long batch the prefix goes cold once processing moves past the early requests, so the documented guidance is to use the **1-hour TTL** for batch workloads. It costs about **2x** base input to write instead of 1.25x, but reads stay at about 0.1x and you collect them across far more requests. Keep the prefix byte-identical across every `custom_id` so they all match the same entry.",
w:"B — caching does apply to batch requests, and the early reads were real. C — carving the work into 60 small batches multiplies writes and adds orchestration without addressing the TTL mismatch. D — the maximum is 4 breakpoints and a 5th returns a 400; more breakpoints would not extend residency anyway."},

{id:1280,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"Before submitting a request with 60 large page images, a service calls `/v1/messages/count_tokens`, gets a successful count, and treats it as a pre-flight check. The subsequent Messages call is rejected. What did the check not prove?",
o:["A. `count_tokens` excludes image content entirely, so the count was meaningless","B. The counting endpoint estimates an image's cost from its dimensions without fully processing it, so a successful count does not establish that the request satisfies the Messages API's request limits","C. `count_tokens` requires `max_tokens`, and omitting it invalidated the result","D. `count_tokens` exercises prompt caching, and a cache expiry between the two calls caused the rejection"],a:1,
e:"`/v1/messages/count_tokens` is **free, has its own RPM limit, and returns a single `input_tokens` estimate** covering messages, system, and tools. For images it estimates cost from **dimensions, without fully processing the bytes**, so an image can count successfully and still be rejected when sent, for example by the per-image size limit, the 8000 px ceiling, or the stricter dimension rule that applies above 20 image and document blocks. Treat counting as a cost and context check, and validate limits separately in your own code.",
w:"A — image cost is included in the count; the estimate is just computed from dimensions. C — `count_tokens` does not require `max_tokens`. D — the counting endpoint does not exercise prompt caching, so cache state is irrelevant to it."},

{id:1281,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A coordinate-sensitive pipeline marks images with `transformations: {\"oversized_image\": \"error\"}` and adds a `count_tokens` pre-flight to catch violations before inference. It reliably catches embedded images but never catches oversized images referenced by `url` or `file_id`. Why?",
o:["A. `count_tokens` ignores the `transformations` field entirely","B. `count_tokens` fetches remote images and validates them, so the problem lies elsewhere","C. Counting honours `transformations` for embedded images, rejecting them exactly as the Messages API would, but it never fetches `url` or `file_id` images, so a marked image from those sources is only checked at Messages time","D. `transformations` is valid only on the counting endpoint and is ignored by Messages"],a:2,
e:"The counting endpoint **honours `transformations` for embedded base64 images**, rejecting them exactly as the Messages API would, which makes it a genuine pre-flight for inline content. But **counting never fetches images supplied by URL or file ID**, so their dimensions are unknown at count time and they are validated only when the message is actually sent. Design accordingly: record dimensions at upload for remote assets and check them in your own code, and reserve the count-based pre-flight for embedded images.",
w:"A — the field is honoured for embedded images, which is why the check works at all. B — remote images are deliberately not fetched during counting, which is the whole cause here. D — `transformations` is a Messages request field; the counting endpoint merely respects it."},

{id:1282,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An invoice pipeline must return a typed object per document, with supplier, invoice number, currency, line items, and total, and downstream code must be able to deserialise it without defensive parsing. What is the current mechanism?",
o:["A. Prefill the assistant turn with an opening brace so the reply starts as JSON","B. Force `tool_choice: {\"type\": \"tool\"}` on a schema-bearing extraction tool and read the arguments","C. Ask in the prompt for JSON only, then validate and retry on parse failure","D. Set `output_config.format` to `{\"type\": \"json_schema\", \"schema\": {...}}`, which uses constrained decoding to guarantee schema-valid output"],a:3,
e:"**Structured outputs** are native: `output_config.format` with `{\"type\": \"json_schema\", \"schema\": {...}}` constrains decoding so the response is schema-valid, with no beta header required. The tool-side equivalent is **`strict: true`** on a tool definition. **Currency note:** this supersedes the older workaround of forcing a schema-bearing tool, and assistant prefill now returns a **400** on current models, so the brace trick is dead. Design the schema to model reality: nullable fields for values that may be unreadable, and enums with an explicit unknown branch.",
w:"A — assistant prefill is rejected with a 400 on current models and must be known only as legacy. B — the forced-tool workaround is superseded, and forced `tool_choice` errors under manual extended thinking and 400s on some models. C — prompt-and-retry is the pre-native pattern: it wastes calls and still offers no guarantee."},

{id:1283,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"A form-extraction service uses a typed schema through structured outputs. Every response parses cleanly, yet on faint scans the totals are sometimes wrong. What does this show about the guarantee?",
o:["A. Constrained decoding guarantees the response matches the schema; it says nothing about whether the values were read correctly, so accuracy needs confidence signals, cross-field checks such as line items summing to the total, and a human review path","B. Adding `strict: true` alongside the schema would enforce value accuracy","C. The wrong values indicate the schema was rejected and the model fell back to free text","D. Raising `max_tokens` would let the model double-check its arithmetic"],a:0,
e:"Structured outputs guarantee **shape, not truth**. Constrained decoding removes parse failures and missing fields, which is a real reliability win, but a misread digit is still a misread digit inside perfectly valid JSON. Accuracy has to be engineered separately: deterministic cross-field validation (line items summing to the total, dates in range, checksum-style rules on identifiers), an explicit low-confidence branch in the schema, and a human review queue for anything failing those checks. The principle is that the model proposes and deterministic code authorises.",
w:"B — `strict: true` is the tool-side way to enforce a schema; it is another shape guarantee, not a correctness one. C — a schema rejection would surface as a request error, not as valid JSON with wrong numbers. D — more output room does not improve perception of the input, and self-checking arithmetic on a misread figure confirms the misreading."},

{id:1284,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A claims intake pipeline processes handwritten forms. Some fields are genuinely illegible, and the extractor currently returns a confident guess for them, which propagates into payments. What is the sound design?",
o:["A. Retry the same image three times and take the majority answer","B. Model the schema so an unreadable field can be returned as null or flagged `illegible`, and route those documents to a human review queue instead of forcing a value","C. Downscale the images so the model commits to fewer fine details","D. Add \"never say you are unsure\" to the system prompt so output stays consistent"],a:1,
e:"If the schema has no way to express \"I could not read this\", a constrained decoder must emit **something**, and that something is a guess. The fix is schema design plus routing: make genuinely optional fields nullable, add an explicit `illegible` or `unknown` branch, ask for a per-field confidence where useful, and send any document with a flagged field to a **human review queue**. Combine with deterministic checks so implausible values are caught even when nothing is flagged. Model the low-confidence path first; it is the path that costs money when it is missing.",
w:"A — three samples of an unreadable field give three guesses, and agreement between them is not evidence. C — reducing resolution removes information and makes confabulation more likely, not less. D — suppressing uncertainty is the opposite of what a payments pipeline needs, and prompt instructions are advisory rather than enforceable."},

{id:1285,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A mobile expense app sends every captured photo straight to the API. About one in six is too blurry or too dark to read, and each of those consumes a full request before failing review. What is the highest-leverage change?",
o:["A. Add more worked examples to the prompt so the model handles poor photos better","B. Send each photo twice and compare the two extractions","C. Gate at capture: score sharpness and exposure on the device, prompt the user to retake while the receipt is still in hand, and only spend an API call on images that pass","D. Move to a high-resolution-tier model, whose larger token budget resolves blur"],a:2,
e:"Information absent from the pixels cannot be recovered downstream, so the cheapest place to fix an unreadable image is **at capture, while the subject is still available**. A client-side quality gate on sharpness, exposure, and framing removes the whole failure class, saves the request, and gives the user immediate feedback instead of a next-day rejection. Pair it with client-side pre-processing (deskew, crop to the document, contrast normalisation) so the images that do pass are the best version of themselves.",
w:"A — prompt examples improve interpretation of readable input and cannot conjure detail that was never captured. B — duplicate extraction of an unreadable image doubles cost for no additional information. D — a bigger token budget preserves detail that exists; it does not remove motion blur or fix underexposure."},

{id:1286,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A release pipeline wants to catch visual regressions between a baseline screenshot and a candidate build screenshot, and to get a human-readable summary of what changed. What is the sound approach?",
o:["A. Rely on Claude to enumerate pixel-exact differences and gate the release on that list","B. Send only the candidate screenshot and describe the baseline in words","C. Send an animated GIF that alternates the two frames so the difference is visible","D. Send both as labelled `image` blocks before the prompt, use a deterministic pixel diff as the pass/fail gate, and use Claude to explain and triage what the diff found"],a:3,
e:"Split the problem by what each component can guarantee. A pixel diff is **exact, cheap, and deterministic**, so it owns the gate. Claude is good at the part a diff is bad at: saying that a button moved below the fold, that contrast now fails, or that a diff is cosmetic. Send both images as labelled blocks (`Baseline:`, `Candidate:`) before the question, image-then-text, so references stay unambiguous. Remember spatial output is approximate, so never let it own the pass/fail decision.",
w:"A — coordinate and localisation output is approximate by design, so a probabilistic component would own a gate that must be exact. B — a verbal description of the baseline discards the comparison signal entirely. C — animated formats are read as a single frame, so the alternation is invisible to the model."},

{id:1287,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An analytics assistant currently screenshots each dashboard chart at 1000x1000 px and asks Claude to summarise the trend. The underlying series is available as CSV from the same service. What is the better input, and why?",
o:["A. Send the numbers as text: the chart image costs 1296 visual tokens and forces value estimation from pixels, while the series itself is cheaper and exact","B. Always send the image, because visual reasoning about trends is more reliable than reading a table","C. Send both, which halves the cost by letting the model skip the harder representation","D. Rasterise the chart at 8000 px so individual data points become readable"],a:0,
e:"Vision is for information that only exists as pixels. When the underlying data is available, sending it as text is both **cheaper** and **exact**: a 1000x1000 chart costs `36 x 36` = 1296 visual tokens and still forces the model to estimate values off a rendered axis. Reserve images for the cases where rendering carries the meaning, such as annotated dashboards, layout questions, or documents with no machine-readable source. The transferable principle: prefer the most structured representation of a fact that you already possess.",
w:"B — reading values from a rendered chart is an estimation task; a table removes the estimation entirely. C — sending both increases input cost rather than halving it, and adds a reconciliation problem. D — anything beyond the tier limits is downscaled before inference, so the extra pixels are discarded and the 8000 px ceiling is a hard rejection point besides."},

{id:1288,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team using the legacy Converse API on Amazon Bedrock finds Claude answers questions about PDF wording but claims not to see any charts or diagrams in the same documents. Token usage is far lower than expected for the page count. What is the cause?",
o:["A. Bedrock does not support PDF documents at all, so text is being pulled from elsewhere","B. In the legacy Converse API, full visual PDF understanding requires citations to be enabled; without it the call falls back to basic text extraction only, which also explains the low token usage. Enabling citations, or using InvokeModel, restores visual analysis","C. Every page must be converted to a JPEG, because document blocks are unsupported on Bedrock","D. `max_tokens` is too low for chart descriptions to fit in the response"],a:1,
e:"On the legacy **Converse** API there are two document modes. Without citations you get **Converse Document Chat**: basic text extraction, no analysis of images, charts, or layout, and a much smaller token footprint, roughly 1,000 tokens for a 3-page PDF. With citations enabled you get **Claude PDF Chat**, which processes each page as both text and image, at roughly 7,000 tokens for the same 3 pages. The usage gap is the diagnostic. **InvokeModel** gives full control without forcing citations.",
w:"A — PDFs are supported on Bedrock; the question is which processing mode the call selects. C — manual rasterisation is a workaround, not a requirement, and it discards the extracted text layer. D — `max_tokens` bounds the response; the charts were never analysed on the input side."},

{id:1289,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which file types can be sent in a `document` block?",
o:["A. Any office document, including .xlsx and .docx, alongside PDF","B. PDF and .docx only","C. PDFs, plus plain text files such as .txt, .csv, and .md uploaded to the Files API with MIME type `text/plain`; binary formats such as .xlsx and .docx are unsupported and must be converted to text or PDF first","D. PDFs only, with everything else sent as base64 `image` blocks"],a:2,
e:"`document` blocks accept **PDFs** and **plain text files** (`.txt`, `.csv`, `.md`), the latter uploaded to the Files API with MIME type **`text/plain`** and referenced by `file_id`. **Binary office formats such as .xlsx and .docx are not supported and must be converted to text or PDF first.** That conversion step is where document pipelines usually break in production, so make it explicit at ingest: detect the type, convert deterministically, and reject or route anything unhandled rather than letting an opaque API error reach the user.",
w:"A — spreadsheet and word-processor binaries are explicitly unsupported in document blocks. B — .docx is unsupported, and plain text is supported, so both halves are wrong. D — plain text files are supported in document blocks, and rendering text as images would be needlessly expensive."},

{id:1290,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"E",
q:"An architecture tool stores its diagrams as SVG and wants Claude to critique them. Sending the SVG as an image block fails. What should the team do?",
o:["A. Send the SVG with `media_type: \"image/svg+xml\"`","B. Send it in a `document` block instead","C. Use a `url` source, which accepts vector formats","D. SVG is not among the supported formats (JPEG, PNG, GIF, WebP), so rasterise it to PNG at a sensible size, or send the SVG markup as text when the markup itself is the subject"],a:3,
e:"Supported image formats are exactly **JPEG, PNG, GIF, and WebP**; SVG is not one of them, and no source type widens that list. Rasterise to PNG at dimensions chosen with the patch formula in mind, so the diagram is legible without paying for pixels that get downscaled away. There is a second option worth remembering: SVG is text, so when the question is about the markup (structure, labels, generated output correctness) you can send the source in a text block instead and skip visual tokens entirely.",
w:"A — declaring an unsupported `media_type` does not make the decoder accept it. B — `document` blocks are for PDFs and plain text files, not vector images. C — the `url` source only changes transport; the format list is unchanged."},

{id:1291,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A pipeline submits 200 `url`-sourced images with `transformations: {\"oversized_image\": \"error\"}` set on each. A request is rejected for an oversized image, but the error does not indicate which one. How should this be handled?",
o:["A. Rejections for `url` and `file_id` images omit the leading content position, so they do not identify which image failed; only embedded base64 images are named by position. Record dimensions at upload and validate them in your own pipeline before submitting","B. `url` sources are unsupported in requests with more than 20 images, which is the real error","C. The error always names the failing `file_id`, so the client is discarding part of the message","D. Enable streaming so the failing image is reported as it is processed"],a:0,
e:"Images supplied by `url` or `file_id` are checked **once their bytes have been fetched**, and those rejections carry the same message **without the leading content position**, so they do not identify the offender. Only **embedded base64** images are named by position. The operational consequence is that remote-asset pipelines must own their own validation: capture width and height at upload time, check them against the tier limits of every model the request names, and fail the individual asset locally where you can attribute the failure.",
w:"B — `url` sources are valid regardless of image count; the many-image rule tightens dimensions rather than banning source types. C — the message deliberately omits the identifier for fetched sources, so nothing is being discarded client-side. D — streaming shapes the response and does nothing for input validation, which happens before generation begins."},

{id:1292,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"Can `transformations` be set on a `document` block to control how its PDF pages are rasterised?",
o:["A. Yes; it applies uniformly to every page rendered from the PDF","B. No: the `document` block does not accept the field, because PDF pages are rasterised server-side at dimensions you do not control. An `image` block nested inside a document's content does accept it, like any other image block","C. Yes, but only when citations are enabled on the document","D. It is accepted on document blocks and silently ignored"],a:1,
e:"`transformations` exists to protect image dimensions you chose, so it is meaningful only where you chose them. **PDF pages are rasterised server-side at dimensions you do not control**, so the `document` block does not accept the field, and this is exactly why coordinate workflows over PDFs require rasterising pages yourself. An `image` block nested inside a document's `content` is an ordinary image block and accepts the field normally. The related consequence: coordinates returned for native PDF pages cannot be reliably mapped back onto the page.",
w:"A — there are no client-controlled page dimensions for the field to constrain. C — citations govern source attribution and have no bearing on rasterisation control. D — silent acceptance would be worse than rejection; the field is not accepted on the block at all."},

{id:1293,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A request contains an image marked `transformations: {\"oversized_image\": \"error\"}` whose header is corrupt, so its dimensions cannot be determined. What happens?",
o:["A. It is passed through unresized, since no resize can be computed","B. It is downscaled to the tier maximum as a conservative default","C. It is rejected, with an error stating that the image's source dimensions could not be determined; no image marked `\"error\"` reaches the model resized","D. The `transformations` field is ignored and the default `\"downsize\"` behaviour applies"],a:2,
e:"The setting's guarantee is absolute: **no image that sets `\"error\"` reaches the model resized**. When dimensions cannot be determined, the platform cannot prove the image would not be resized, so it **rejects rather than passes through**, with a distinct message reporting that the source dimensions could not be determined rather than the usual resize message. That is fail-closed design, and it is what makes the flag usable as a safety property in coordinate pipelines rather than merely a hint.",
w:"A — passing through would silently break the very guarantee the flag exists to provide. B — a conservative downscale is still a resize, which the flag forbids. D — the field is honoured; falling back to the default would make the guarantee conditional on file health."},

{id:1294,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"One request carries a screenshot whose exact dimensions matter because Claude will return click coordinates for it, plus a decorative brand logo where resizing is harmless. How should the oversized-image behaviour be configured?",
o:["A. It is a per-request setting, so both images must share one mode","B. Marking any image `\"error\"` makes every image in the request error if it would be resized","C. It must be declared once at the top level of the request body","D. The setting is per image block, and each block is checked only against its own value, so mark the screenshot `\"error\"` and leave the logo at the default `\"downsize\"`"],a:3,
e:"`transformations` is a **per-image-block** setting, and **each block is checked only against its own value**. That is what makes it practical: mark the images whose dimensions are load-bearing, and let everything decorative continue to be downscaled automatically, in one request. Whichever mode applies, padding, format conversion, and orientation correction proceed as usual, and the hard ceilings (8000 px, and the stricter many-image dimension limit) remain separate rejections that no setting bypasses.",
w:"A — treating it as request-wide forces you to choose between protection and convenience unnecessarily. B — one marked block does not change how other blocks are evaluated. C — there is no top-level equivalent; the field lives on the image content block."},

{id:1295,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What is required to get high-resolution image handling on a model that supports it?",
o:["A. Nothing: high-resolution support is automatic on the models listed for that tier and needs no beta header or client-side opt-in","B. Send the feature name in the `anthropic-beta` header","C. Request it through the image block's `transformations` field","D. Upload the image through the Files API, which unlocks the higher tier"],a:0,
e:"High-resolution handling is a **property of the model, applied automatically**, with no beta header and no client-side opt-in: the newest models carry roughly a 2576 px edge limit and a 4784 visual-token budget, while others sit at about 1568 px and 1568 tokens. The practical consequence is that a model swap silently changes both fidelity and cost, since the same large image can consume up to about three times the visual tokens. Treat tier as an input to cost modelling and to any coordinate arithmetic, and downsample deliberately when you do not need the detail.",
w:"B — no beta header gates it, and an invalid or unentitled beta name returns a 400 anyway. C — `transformations` chooses between resizing and erroring; it does not select a resolution tier. D — the Files API is transport and storage and confers no capability."},

{id:1296,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A browser-automation agent runs 60 steps, each adding one screenshot to the history. Input token spend per turn climbs steadily even on turns where the screenshot is nearly identical to the last. What is the right mental model and mitigation?",
o:["A. Images in history are billed once, on the turn they are introduced, so the growth must come from text","B. Every image in the resent history is billed as input visual tokens on each turn, so cache the stable prefix with `cache_control` and drop or downsample screenshots the agent no longer needs","C. Only text is re-billed; image blocks are cached automatically after first use","D. Lower `max_tokens` to reduce the per-turn input cost"],a:1,
e:"The API is **stateless**: each turn resends the accumulated history, and every image in it is re-billed as input visual tokens. At roughly 1,500 tokens per screenshot, 60 accumulated frames is around 90,000 input tokens per turn before any text. Two mitigations compose. **Prompt caching** on the stable prefix turns repeated reads into about 0.1x, and history hygiene, dropping or downsampling superseded screenshots, removes the tokens outright. Referencing by `file_id` shrinks the payload but not the token bill.",
w:"A — there is no server-side memory to bill against once, so history is charged again on every request. C — caching is opt-in via `cache_control` breakpoints on a byte-identical prefix, never automatic per block. D — `max_tokens` bounds generated output and never appears in input accounting."},

{id:1297,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A product manager asks Claude to remove the background from a product photo and return the edited PNG. What is the correct response?",
o:["A. Return the edited image as base64 in a content block with a mask attached","B. Ask for an SVG rendering of the subject instead","C. Claude is an image-understanding model only: it cannot generate, produce, edit, manipulate, or create images. Have Claude describe or locate the region and do the pixel work in deterministic code or a dedicated generation model","D. Write the edited file back through the Files API"],a:2,
e:"The boundary is broader than most people remember: Claude **cannot generate images, and cannot edit or manipulate them either**. Its output is text (and tool calls). The productive architecture keeps Claude on judgement and puts pixels elsewhere: ask it for the subject's bounding box in absolute pixel coordinates, or for a critique of a candidate cut-out, then let an image library or a dedicated generation model perform the operation. That also gives you a deterministic, testable, and far cheaper pixel stage.",
w:"A — the model produces no image content blocks in its response, so there is nothing to return. B — producing a vector rendering of the subject is still image generation. D — the Files API stores what your code uploads; it does not let the model author image bytes."},

{id:1298,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A 300-page PDF ingestion job runs fine against a large-context model. After routing the same job to a cheaper 200k-context model for cost reasons, every request fails validation before any generation happens. What changed?",
o:["A. The PDF was re-encoded during the migration and is no longer a standard file","B. The 32 MB request size limit is lower on smaller models","C. Prompt caching does not apply on 200k-context models, and the failure is a cache error","D. The page ceiling depends on the request's context window: 600 pages drops to 100 when the context window is under 1M tokens, so a 300-page document now fails. The per-request image ceiling tightens on small-context models in the same way"],a:3,
e:"PDF page limits are **conditional on the request's context window**: 600 pages, falling to **100 when the context window is under 1M tokens**. The per-request image ceiling behaves analogously, 100 on 200k-context models against 600 on others. So model selection is not only a price and capability decision; it moves hard input ceilings. Either keep large documents on a large-context model or chunk to under 100 pages per request and aggregate in your own code, which is often cheaper overall anyway.",
w:"A — a re-encoding fault would fail on both models, and nothing about routing rewrites the file. B — the 32 MB request size limit is not a function of the model's context window; it varies by platform. C — caching is available and a cache miss degrades cost, never causing a pre-generation validation failure."},

{id:1299,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What is a visual token?",
o:["A. A 28x28-pixel patch of the image, the unit in which image input is counted","B. A single pixel of the image","C. A 750-pixel block, from which the legacy cost formula was derived","D. An output token generated while the model describes an image"],a:0,
e:"Claude views images **in patches instead of pixels**, and each **28x28-pixel patch is one visual token**, billed as input. An image therefore costs `⌈width/28⌉ x ⌈height/28⌉`, computed on the dimensions actually processed after any downscaling, which is why the tier's visual-token budget doubles as a per-image cost ceiling. **Currency note:** the older `(width x height)/750` approximation is obsolete; keeping it in a cost model produces estimates that drift with aspect ratio and miss the tier cap entirely.",
w:"B — per-pixel accounting would make even small images ruinously expensive; the patch is the unit. C — 750 belongs to the superseded divide-by-750 approximation and is not a block size. D — visual tokens are input, counted before generation begins."},

{id:1300,d:"D1",s:"Vision & Multimodal",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team running Claude on Microsoft Foundry with an Azure-hosted deployment ports a working document pipeline that uploads PDFs and references them by `file_id`. Every request fails. What is the constraint?",
o:["A. On Microsoft Foundry the Files API is not supported for deployments hosted on Azure, so documents must be supplied as `base64` or `url` sources","B. Microsoft Foundry does not offer Claude models at all","C. PDFs are unsupported outside the first-party Claude API","D. `file_id` references require citations to be enabled on partner platforms"],a:0,
e:"Source-type availability is **platform-specific**, and this is the detail that breaks ports. Claude is offered on Amazon Bedrock, Google Cloud, and Microsoft Foundry as well as first-party, but on **Microsoft Foundry the Files API is not supported for deployments hosted on Azure**, and on **Amazon Bedrock and Google Cloud only `base64` sources are available** for images and documents. Abstract the source-construction step behind one interface in your code so a platform move changes a single implementation rather than every call site.",
w:"B — Claude is offered on Microsoft Foundry; the restriction is on the Files API for Azure-hosted deployments. C — PDF processing is supported on the Claude API, Bedrock, Google Cloud, and Microsoft Foundry. D — citations affect source attribution and the legacy Converse document mode, not whether `file_id` references resolve."}
);
